// Original educational content (English edition). Not ISACA text.
window.COBIT_CONTROLS_EN = window.COBIT_CONTROLS_EN || [];
window.COBIT_CONTROLS_EN.push(...
[
{"objective":"DSS01","purpose":"Running IT operations (job scheduling, infrastructure monitoring, outsourced services, environment and facilities) in a predictable and repeatable manner ensures that the systems supporting business processes operate accurately and on time. Where operational discipline is weak, incomplete batch jobs, undetected capacity issues or environmental failures translate directly into business disruption and data integrity issues.","auditFocus":"The senior auditor verifies, based on evidence, that scheduled jobs processing financial and other business-critical data are defined and changed only with authorization, and that failures and deviations are monitored and resolved. The auditor also assesses, through independent assurance reports and complementary user entity controls, whether outsourced service providers and data center controls meet the organization’s assurance expectations.","keyRisks":["Incomplete or inaccurate data processing because failed or skipped scheduled jobs go undetected","Assurance gaps over outsourced services and failure to operate complementary user entity controls","Environmental threats such as power loss, cooling failure or fire causing prolonged outages of critical infrastructure"],"practices":[
{"code":"DSS01.01","name":"Perform operational procedures.","title":"Operating procedures and job scheduling management","expectation":"Routine operational activities (scheduled jobs, batch job chains, end-of-day processing, shift handovers) are based on documented procedures, and job definitions in the scheduling tool are updated only by authorized personnel through the change management process. Failed or delayed jobs are monitored against thresholds and a responsibility matrix defined by the organization; reruns are logged and justified. Development teams’ authority to modify production job definitions is segregated from that of the operations team.","design":"The auditor inspects whether authority to create and modify jobs in the scheduling tool is restricted, whether failure alerts are routed to defined recipients and whether an approval rule has been designed for reruns.","operating":"The complete job execution history for the audit period is extracted from the scheduler and its completeness is validated; for a risk-based selection of failed jobs and manual interventions, the incident record, resolution and approval trail are inspected. Changes to job definitions are matched to change records.","evidence":["Job execution history and failure list for the audit period from the job scheduling tool","List of users with authority to define and modify jobs in the scheduler","Operations manual and shift handover records","Incident records raised for failed jobs and rerun approvals"],"findings":["No incident records were raised for some failed overnight jobs, and the rationale for reruns was not documented.","Developer accounts in the job scheduling tool had authority to modify production job definitions."],"iso":["5.37","8.32"],"clauses":["8.1"],"itgc":"operations"},
{"code":"DSS01.02","name":"Manage outsourced I&T services.","title":"Operational assurance over outsourced IT services","expectation":"For outsourced operations such as hosting, cloud and managed services, contracts define requirements for security, service levels, right to audit and incident notification. The organization obtains an independent assurance report or equivalent evidence from the provider at a frequency defined by the organization, evaluates the report’s scope and exceptions, and operates on its side the complementary user entity controls set out in the report. Service performance is reviewed regularly by a designated service owner.","design":"The auditor evaluates whether the supplier management procedure includes steps for obtaining assurance, evaluating reports and assigning ownership of complementary user entity controls.","operating":"The list of critical outsourced services is reconciled to the contract inventory; for a risk-based selection of providers, the auditor verifies through evidence that the assurance report covers the period, that exceptions have been evaluated and that complementary controls are operating.","evidence":["Inventory of outsourced services and the security, SLA and right-to-audit clauses of the contracts","Providers’ independent assurance reports and the organization’s report evaluation notes","Complementary user entity controls mapping and evidence of their operation","Minutes of service level review meetings"],"findings":["The hosting provider’s assurance report covered only part of the audit period, and no additional assurance was obtained for the gap.","Complementary user entity controls specified in the assurance report had not been assigned owners within the organization."],"iso":["5.19","5.20","5.22","5.23"],"clauses":["8.1"],"itgc":"operations"},
{"code":"DSS01.03","name":"Monitor I&T infrastructure.","title":"Infrastructure monitoring and capacity tracking","expectation":"Critical server, database, network and cloud components are continuously monitored through a central monitoring tool; availability, performance and capacity thresholds are set at values defined by the organization according to the criticality of the service. Threshold breaches generate automatic alerts that are linked to incident management; assets excluded from monitoring are justified. Capacity trends are reported periodically and provide input to planning.","design":"The auditor inspects whether the monitoring scope includes all critical components in the asset inventory and whether alerts are linked to an owned resolution workflow.","operating":"The asset inventory is compared with the list of assets in the monitoring tool to identify coverage gaps; critical alerts selected from the period are traced to confirm that they resulted in incident records and were addressed on a timely basis.","evidence":["Comparison of the monitored asset list in the monitoring tool with the asset inventory","Threshold definitions and alert routing rules","Critical alerts for the period and related incident records","Capacity trend reports"],"findings":["Some newly deployed production servers had not been added to the monitoring tool.","Disk utilization alerts were routed to an unowned shared mailbox and were not followed up."],"iso":["8.6","8.16"],"clauses":["9.1"],"itgc":"operations"},
{"code":"DSS01.04","name":"Manage the environment.","title":"Data center environmental protection measures","expectation":"Measures for power continuity (UPS, generator), cooling, humidity, fire detection and suppression, and water leak detection have been installed in data centers and server rooms based on a risk assessment. Equipment is maintained in line with manufacturer recommendations and a maintenance plan defined by the organization, and alarms are relayed to a continuously monitored point. Components such as generators and UPS units are periodically tested under load.","design":"The auditor evaluates whether environmental threats are addressed in the risk assessment and whether detection, alarm and response measures are defined for each threat.","operating":"Maintenance and test records for the period are compared with the maintenance plan; through an authorized on-site observation, the condition of alarm panels, sensors and the suppression system is inspected, and follow-up of events selected from alarm logs is verified.","evidence":["Maintenance contracts and service reports for UPS, generator, air conditioning and fire suppression systems","Generator and UPS load test records","Environmental monitoring system alarm logs","Data center on-site observation notes"],"findings":["The generator load test was not performed in the planned period, and the related maintenance report was not available.","Temperature alarms in the server room were not relayed to any responsible person outside working hours."],"iso":["7.5","7.11","7.13"],"clauses":["6.1"],"itgc":"operations"},
{"code":"DSS01.05","name":"Manage facilities.","title":"IT facilities management and physical infrastructure organization","expectation":"Layout, cabling standards, occupational health and safety requirements and facility maintenance for the data center, cabinets and cabling have been planned, with responsibilities assigned. Cables are protected and labeled, facility changes are made with approval and facility issues are logged. In shared (colocation) facilities, the allocation of responsibilities with the provider is documented in writing.","design":"The auditor inspects whether the facilities management procedure covers the maintenance plan, cabling standard, facility change approval and provider responsibilities.","operating":"A risk-based sample is selected from facility maintenance and change records to verify the approval and completion trail; cabling organization, labeling and equipment layout are inspected through on-site observation.","evidence":["Facility maintenance plan and completed work orders","Cabling diagrams and labeling standard","Colocation contract and responsibility matrix","On-site observation checklist"],"findings":["Unlabeled and undocumented cable connections were observed in cabinets.","Some facility maintenance work orders remained open without evidence of completion."],"iso":["7.8","7.12","7.13"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"operations"}
]},
{"objective":"DSS02","purpose":"Consistently recording and prioritizing user requests and incidents limits the business impact of disruptions and enables normal service to be restored quickly. This process is also the foundation for ensuring that control-relevant requests, such as access and software installation, are fulfilled in an approved and traceable manner.","auditFocus":"The auditor verifies, based on evidence, that incidents are recorded completely and accurately, that prioritization is based on business impact and that critical incidents are resolved on a timely basis through defined escalation. Particular attention is given to whether security incidents are routed to a separate response process and whether service requests are fulfilled only after approval.","keyRisks":["Late detection of critical outages because incidents are not recorded or are incorrectly prioritized","Fulfillment of unapproved service requests (such as access or software installation)","Security breaches being handled as routine faults and evidence being lost"],"practices":[
{"code":"DSS02.01","name":"Define classification schemes for incidents and service requests.","title":"Incident and request classification and priority scheme","expectation":"Category, impact and urgency criteria, a priority matrix and target response and resolution times for each priority level are defined by the organization for incidents and service requests and are aligned with service level agreements. The concepts of security incident, major incident and problem are clearly distinguished from one another, and escalation rules are documented.","design":"The auditor evaluates whether the scheme is based on business impact, includes criteria to distinguish security incidents and is consistent with SLA targets.","operating":"The category and priority configuration in the service desk tool is compared with the approved scheme; for selected records, reperformance is used to check whether priority was assigned in accordance with the scheme.","evidence":["Approved incident and request classification scheme and priority matrix","Screenshots of the service desk tool configuration","Service level agreements","Major incident and security incident definition document"],"findings":["The priority matrix configured in the tool differed from the matrix in the approved procedure.","No category or criteria had been defined to distinguish security incidents."],"iso":["5.24","5.25"],"clauses":["7.5"],"itgc":"operations"},
{"code":"DSS02.02","name":"Record, classify and prioritize requests and incidents.","title":"Recording, classifying and prioritizing requests and incidents","expectation":"All incidents and requests, regardless of the channel through which they are received (telephone, email, portal or monitoring alert), are recorded in a single tool, classified according to the scheme and assigned a priority. Records include date and time, affected service, reporting user and assigned group; subsequent priority changes are justified and traceable.","design":"The auditor inspects whether all intake channels are connected to the recording tool and whether mandatory fields are enforced by the tool.","operating":"A full extract of records for the period is obtained and the completion of mandatory fields is assessed through data analytics; samples selected from monitoring alerts are traced to confirm that they resulted in records, and priority changes are inspected for justification.","evidence":["Extract of incident and request records for the period","Mapping of monitoring alerts to resulting records","Audit trail of priority changes","Recording procedure"],"findings":["Some incidents reported by telephone were resolved without being recorded.","The affected service field was left blank in a portion of the records."],"iso":["5.25","6.8"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"operations"},
{"code":"DSS02.03","name":"Verify, approve and fulfill service requests.","title":"Verifying, approving and fulfilling service requests","expectation":"For each standard request type, such as access, hardware and software installation, authorized approvers and predefined fulfillment steps are specified. A request is not fulfilled until the requester’s identity and the appropriateness of the request have been verified and the required approval has been obtained; requests that constitute changes are routed to change management.","design":"The auditor evaluates whether the approval workflow is enforced in the tool for each type in the request catalog and whether it is designed so that requesters cannot approve their own requests.","operating":"A risk-based sample of requests fulfilled during the period is selected, particularly of the access and software installation types; timestamps are used to verify that approval was given before fulfillment and by an authorized person.","evidence":["Service request catalog and approval matrix","Approval and completion timestamps of fulfilled requests","Workflow configuration","Approver authority list"],"findings":["Some software installation requests were fulfilled before the approval date.","Workflows existed in which the requester could also act as the approver."],"iso":["5.18"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"operations"},
{"code":"DSS02.04","name":"Investigate, diagnose and allocate incidents.","title":"Investigating, diagnosing and assigning incidents to the right group","expectation":"Incidents are diagnosed at first level using known error records and the knowledge base; those that cannot be resolved are assigned to specialist groups or suppliers in accordance with defined escalation rules. Records suspected of being security incidents are transferred to the security team and evidence integrity is preserved; all assignments and handovers are traceable within the record.","design":"The auditor inspects whether escalation and assignment rules, a handover step for suspected security incidents and known error sources are integrated into the process.","operating":"For a sample of high-priority incidents, the record history is used to verify assignment times, compliance of escalation with the rules and timely transfer of security-related incidents to the security team.","evidence":["Escalation procedure and assignment rules","Assignment and escalation history of selected incidents","List of incidents handed over to the security team","Knowledge base and known error records"],"findings":["Some records involving suspected phishing were closed at the service desk without being transferred to the security team.","High-priority incidents were escalated after the defined time limit had been exceeded."],"iso":["5.25","5.26","5.28"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"operations"},
{"code":"DSS02.05","name":"Resolve and recover from incidents.","title":"Resolving incidents and restoring service","expectation":"Incident resolutions are documented, and fixes affecting the production environment are made through change management; emergency interventions are subsequently approved by authorized personnel. Service restoration is confirmed through user confirmation or monitoring data; for major incidents, coordination and communication are led by a defined role, and the impact on data integrity is assessed separately.","design":"The auditor evaluates whether the recording of resolution steps, retrospective approval of emergency changes and a major incident coordination role have been designed.","operating":"For a sample of major incidents and incidents involving intervention in production, the resolution steps, related emergency change records and confirmation of service restoration are inspected.","evidence":["Major incident reports and communication records","Emergency change records linked to incidents and retrospective approvals","Resolution notes and evidence of restoration confirmation","Major incident management procedure"],"findings":["Configuration changes made in production during incident resolution were not linked to change records.","Data integrity impact was not assessed for some major incidents."],"iso":["5.26","8.32"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"operations"},
{"code":"DSS02.06","name":"Close service requests and incidents.","title":"Closing requests and incidents","expectation":"Records are closed only after resolution has been verified, either with user confirmation or under an automatic closure rule defined by the organization. A resolution code and cause category are entered at closure; recurring incidents are flagged for transfer to problem management.","design":"The auditor inspects whether closure criteria and mandatory fields such as the resolution code are enforced in the tool.","operating":"For a sample of closed records, resolution verification and the closure code are inspected; reopened records and clusters of recurring records for the same issue are analyzed using the period’s data.","evidence":["Closure procedure and automatic closure rule","Sample closed records and user confirmations","Reopened records report","Closure code distribution analysis"],"findings":["Some records were closed without a resolution note being entered.","Records repeatedly opened for the same issue were not transferred to problem management."],"iso":["5.27"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"operations"},
{"code":"DSS02.07","name":"Track status and produce reports.","title":"Status tracking and incident and request performance reporting","expectation":"Aging of open records, SLA compliance, trends and recurring incidents are reported regularly and reviewed by service owners and management. Report data is kept consistent with the source tool, and actions defined for SLA breaches are tracked through to closure.","design":"The auditor evaluates whether the report set reaches decision-makers and includes a follow-up mechanism for SLA breaches.","operating":"Reports for selected periods are validated by recalculating them from source tool data; minutes of review meetings are inspected for follow-up of actions.","evidence":["Periodic incident and request performance reports","Raw data extracted from the source tool","Minutes of service review meetings","SLA breach action list"],"findings":["The reported SLA compliance rate was inconsistent with the value recalculated from raw data.","Long-outstanding open records were not tracked in the reports."],"iso":[],"clauses":["9.1"],"itgc":""}
]},
{"objective":"DSS03","purpose":"Problem management delivers permanent solutions by identifying the root causes of recurring incidents, reducing the frequency and cost of service disruptions. An organization that responds to incidents only with workarounds experiences the same failures repeatedly and uses its resources inefficiently.","auditFocus":"The auditor examines whether problem records are systematically derived from incident data, whether root cause analyses are actually performed and whether permanent fixes are implemented through change management. The auditor also assesses whether known errors are shared with the service desk and whether closed problems have actually reduced the related incidents.","keyRisks":["Ongoing disruptions because the root cause of recurring incidents is not identified","Permanent fixes being implemented outside change management","Extended resolution times because known errors and workarounds are not shared"],"practices":[
{"code":"DSS03.01","name":"Identify and classify problems.","title":"Identifying and classifying problems","expectation":"Problem records are opened based on defined triggers such as recurring incidents, major incidents, supplier notifications and trend analyses, are prioritized by impact and urgency, and are linked to the related incident records. An owner is assigned to each problem.","design":"The auditor evaluates whether triggers for opening problems are defined, for example a rule requiring a problem record after every major incident.","operating":"Major incidents and recurring incident clusters in the period are identified through data analytics and compared with problem records to determine whether problem records were opened for them.","evidence":["Problem management procedure and trigger definitions","Problem record list and related incidents","Major incident list","Incident trend analysis"],"findings":["No problem record was opened for some major incidents during the period.","Problem records were not linked to the related incident records."],"iso":["5.27"],"clauses":["10.2"],"itgc":"operations"},
{"code":"DSS03.02","name":"Investigate and diagnose problems.","title":"Root cause analysis and diagnosis","expectation":"Problems are investigated using a structured root cause analysis method such as timeline analysis, five whys or fishbone diagrams, together with the relevant technical specialists and, where necessary, suppliers. Analysis results, including contributing control weaknesses, are documented in the problem record.","design":"The auditor inspects whether a method, responsible party and documentation template are defined for root cause analysis.","operating":"For selected problem records, the auditor inspects whether the analysis was actually performed and documented, whether conclusions are supported by evidence and whether they go beyond restating the symptom.","evidence":["Root cause analysis template and completed reports","Analysis notes in problem records","Supplier technical analysis reports","Analysis meeting records"],"findings":["Problem records were identified in which the root cause field merely restated the symptom.","Some problems remained open for extended periods without any analysis being performed."],"iso":["5.27"],"clauses":["10.2"],"itgc":"operations"},
{"code":"DSS03.03","name":"Raise known errors.","title":"Recording known errors and sharing workarounds","expectation":"Problems whose root cause has been identified but whose permanent fix has not yet been implemented are recorded as known errors, and workarounds are published in a knowledge base accessible to the service desk. Known error records are kept up to date according to the status of the permanent fix.","design":"The auditor inspects whether known error records are integrated with the service desk tool and whether responsibility for updating them is defined.","operating":"The auditor verifies that selected known error records are published in the knowledge base and used in related incidents, and that those for which a permanent fix has been implemented have been closed.","evidence":["Extract of known error records","Knowledge base articles","Incident records linked to known errors","Definition of update responsibility"],"findings":["Workarounds were known only within the specialist team and were not included in the knowledge base.","Errors for which permanent fixes had been implemented remained open in the known error list."],"iso":["5.27"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"operations"},
{"code":"DSS03.04","name":"Resolve and close problems.","title":"Permanent resolution and closure of problems","expectation":"Permanent fixes are tested, approved and implemented through change management; a problem is closed after the effectiveness of the fix has been confirmed over an observation period defined by the organization. For problems not resolved due to cost or risk, risk acceptance is granted by authorized personnel.","design":"The auditor inspects whether problem closure is linked to a change record and effectiveness verification, and whether authority for risk acceptance is defined.","operating":"For a sample of closed problems, the related change record, approval and any recurrence of incidents after closure are inspected; for problems closed through risk acceptance, the approver’s authority is checked.","evidence":["Closed problem records and linked change records","Post-closure incident recurrence analysis","Risk acceptance forms","Problem closure criteria"],"findings":["Incidents linked to some closed problems continued to recur after closure.","Problems with deferred resolution were closed without risk acceptance being obtained."],"iso":["5.27","8.32"],"clauses":["10.2"],"itgc":"operations"},
{"code":"DSS03.05","name":"Perform proactive problem management.","title":"Proactive problem management and trend analysis","expectation":"Incident, monitoring and capacity data are analyzed at intervals defined by the organization to identify weaknesses that have not yet caused disruptions; findings are converted into improvement actions with owners and target dates. Indicators such as open problem age and recurrence rate are reported to management.","design":"The auditor inspects whether an owner, frequency and action tracking mechanism are defined for trend analysis.","operating":"Trend analysis reports for the period and the completion status of the resulting actions are inspected; evidence is used to verify that selected actions were actually implemented.","evidence":["Trend analysis reports","Proactive improvement action list","Problem management performance indicators","Management review records"],"findings":["Trend analysis reported only incident counts and did not generate actions.","Identified improvement actions had no owner or target date."],"iso":["5.27"],"clauses":["9.1","10.1"],"itgc":""}
]},
{"objective":"DSS04","purpose":"Continuity management ensures that, in the event of a serious disruption or disaster, critical business processes and the IT services supporting them are restored within an acceptable time and with acceptable data loss. Where recovery objectives are not based on a business impact analysis or plans are not tested, the fact that a plan does not work only becomes apparent during a crisis.","auditFocus":"The auditor examines whether RTO and RPO values are derived from the business impact analysis, whether technical recovery capability meets these objectives and whether this is demonstrated through realistic testing. Priority areas include backup monitoring, in particular restore testing, and the incorporation of test findings into the plans.","keyRisks":["Critical services being restored within an unacceptable timeframe because recovery objectives are not based on a business impact analysis","Backups that cannot be restored because they are incomplete, corrupted or affected by ransomware","Lack of coordination during a crisis due to untested or outdated plans"],"practices":[
{"code":"DSS04.01","name":"Define the business continuity policy, objectives and scope.","title":"Continuity policy, scope and recovery objectives","expectation":"A business continuity policy approved by senior management defines scope, roles and governance. Critical business processes and dependent IT services are identified through a business impact analysis; the RTO and RPO for each are set by business owners based on business impact and updated at intervals defined by the organization or upon significant changes.","design":"The auditor evaluates whether the business impact analysis covers application, infrastructure and supplier dependencies and whether RTO/RPO values are approved by business owners.","operating":"The current business impact analysis and approval records are inspected; the critical application inventory is compared with the service list in the analysis to determine whether any critical systems have been left out of scope.","evidence":["Approved business continuity policy","Business impact analysis report and RTO/RPO table","Business owner approval records","Critical application and dependency inventory"],"findings":["RTO/RPO values were set solely by IT rather than by business owners and were not supported by a business impact analysis.","A recently deployed critical application was not included in the business impact analysis."],"iso":["5.29","5.30"],"clauses":["4.3","5.2","6.1"],"itgc":"operations"},
{"code":"DSS04.02","name":"Maintain business resilience.","title":"Defining and maintaining the resilience strategy","expectation":"Single points of failure are identified for critical services, and options such as redundant infrastructure, a secondary data center, cloud regions, alternative suppliers or manual interim working are selected in proportion to business impact, taking cost and benefit into account. The selected strategy is approved by management and residual risks are explicitly accepted.","design":"The auditor inspects whether strategy selection is based on RTO/RPO and risk assessment and whether single points of failure have been identified.","operating":"For a sample of critical services, the auditor verifies that architecture and redundancy arrangements are consistent with the approved strategy and that acceptance records exist for residual risks.","evidence":["Continuity strategy document and management approval","Critical service architecture diagrams and redundancy arrangements","Single point of failure analysis","Risk acceptance records"],"findings":["A critical service ran in a single data center, and there was no record that this risk had been accepted.","The continuity capability of critical suppliers was not assessed when the strategy was defined."],"iso":["5.30","8.14"],"clauses":["6.1"],"itgc":"operations"},
{"code":"DSS04.03","name":"Develop and implement a business continuity response.","title":"Developing business continuity and disaster recovery plans","expectation":"The BCP and DRP cover the crisis management team, activation criteria, communication chain, system recovery sequence, alternative working arrangements and supplier contacts. Technical recovery procedures are written step by step; plan owners are designated and plans are stored so that they remain accessible even if primary systems are unavailable.","design":"The auditor evaluates whether the plans include, for each critical service, the recovery steps, the dependency sequence and how the RTO/RPO target will be achieved.","operating":"Plans for selected critical services are inspected to verify that contact lists, system names and procedures are consistent with the current infrastructure; the auditor checks that the plan is accessible outside the primary environment.","evidence":["Approved BCP and DRP documents","Crisis management team and contact list","Technical recovery procedures (runbooks)","Evidence that plans are held at an alternative storage location"],"findings":["The DRP contained names of servers no longer in use and of personnel who had left the organization.","Plans were stored only on a file server that could itself be affected by an outage."],"iso":["5.29","5.30"],"clauses":["8.1"],"itgc":"operations"},
{"code":"DSS04.04","name":"Exercise, test and review the business continuity plan (BCP) and disaster response plan (DRP).","title":"BCP and DRP testing and evaluation of results","expectation":"Plans are tested at different levels, from tabletop exercises to technical failover, under a test program defined by the organization. Test objectives are defined in advance, actual recovery time and data loss are compared with RTO/RPO, business users participate in application validation, and corrective actions for deviations are tracked.","design":"The auditor evaluates whether the test program covers all critical services within a reasonable cycle and whether success criteria are tied to RTO/RPO objectives.","operating":"In the test reports for the period, the comparison of measured times against objectives, the participants and the closure of actions are verified; tests planned in the program but not performed are identified.","evidence":["Test program and scope","Test plans and success criteria","Test result reports including measured recovery time and data loss","Test findings action tracking list"],"findings":["The recovery time measured in the DR test exceeded the objective, but no corrective action was defined.","Tests were performed only at infrastructure level, and business users did not participate in application validation."],"iso":["5.30"],"clauses":["9.1"],"itgc":"operations"},
{"code":"DSS04.05","name":"Review, maintain and improve the continuity plans.","title":"Keeping continuity plans current and improving them","expectation":"Plans are updated in response to triggers such as test results, organizational changes, new systems and actual disruption experience; version control is applied and changes are approved by the plan owner. Management reviews continuity readiness at intervals defined by the organization.","design":"The auditor inspects whether update triggers are linked to change management, for example whether a plan update is mandatory when a new critical system is deployed.","operating":"The plan version history is compared with significant changes and test findings during the period to verify that updates were made on a timely basis.","evidence":["Plan version history and approvals","Management review minutes","Evidence that test findings were reflected in the plan","Continuity impact assessments in change records"],"findings":["The DRP was not updated after a significant infrastructure migration.","Plan changes were neither version-controlled nor approved."],"iso":["5.29","5.30"],"clauses":["9.3","10.1"],"itgc":"operations"},
{"code":"DSS04.06","name":"Conduct continuity plan training.","title":"Training for continuity roles","expectation":"Personnel in crisis team and recovery roles receive training appropriate to their responsibilities; deputies have been designated and trained for key roles. Training records are maintained and new appointees are trained within a timeframe defined by the organization.","design":"The auditor inspects whether training needs are defined by role and whether deputy planning has been performed.","operating":"The list of roles in the plan is compared with training records to identify any untrained role holders; interviews with selected individuals are used to assess their command of their roles.","evidence":["Role-based training plan","Training attendance records","Crisis team role and deputy list","Exercise attendance records"],"findings":["Some deputy roles in the crisis team had never received training.","Training records were not maintained and attendance could not be verified."],"iso":["6.3"],"clauses":["7.2","7.3"],"itgc":""},
{"code":"DSS04.07","name":"Manage backup arrangements.","title":"Backups, backup protection and restore testing","expectation":"Backup scope, frequency and retention periods are defined by the organization based on RPO, retention obligations and data classification. Backup job results are monitored regularly and failures are handled as incidents; backups are encrypted, at least one copy is kept logically or physically separate (immutable or offline), and restore tests are performed as planned with results documented.","design":"The auditor evaluates whether the backup policy covers all critical systems, whether access to the backup environment is restricted and whether restore testing is mandatory.","operating":"The critical system inventory is compared with the job list in the backup tool; failures and their follow-up are inspected in the backup job history across the period, and for samples selected from restore test records, data integrity and restore time are verified.","evidence":["Backup policy and system-level backup matrix","Backup tool job history and error logs","Restore test records and validation results","List of users authorized to access the backup environment and configuration of immutable or offline copies"],"findings":["Some critical databases were not included in the backup job.","Restore tests were performed only at single-file level, and a full system recovery had never been attempted."],"iso":["8.13","5.30"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"operations"},
{"code":"DSS04.08","name":"Conduct post-resumption review.","title":"Post-disruption review","expectation":"After every actual disruption or serious incident in which the plan was activated, a structured review is performed once the service has returned to normal operation. The effectiveness of the plan, achievement of objectives, and communication and decision-making processes are evaluated; lessons learned are fed into the plan and into owned actions.","design":"The auditor inspects whether a post-disruption review is mandatory and whether its results are linked to plan updates.","operating":"Plan activations and major disruptions during the period are listed to verify that a review report exists for each and that the resulting actions have been closed.","evidence":["Plan activation records","Post-disruption review reports","Lessons learned action list","Updated plan versions"],"findings":["No review was performed following an actual data center outage.","Review actions were left without owners and were not reflected in the plan."],"iso":["5.27","5.29"],"clauses":["10.2"],"itgc":"operations"}
]},
{"objective":"DSS05","purpose":"Security services protect information assets in day-to-day operations through malware, network, endpoint, identity and access, physical access and security monitoring controls. Weaknesses in this area directly open the door to incidents such as unauthorized access, data leakage and ransomware, and undermine the reliability of the systems supporting financial reporting.","auditFocus":"The senior auditor prioritizes testing of the timely provisioning and removal of access for joiners, movers and leavers, the restriction and monitoring of privileged access, and the effectiveness of periodic user access reviews. In addition, the auditor assesses the collection and monitoring of security logs, the risk-based remediation of vulnerabilities and the coverage of endpoint and network protections.","keyRisks":["Failure to remove the access of leavers or movers on a timely basis, and misuse of privileged accounts","Malware spreading through unpatched vulnerabilities and endpoints outside protection coverage","Late detection of security incidents due to missing logs or lack of monitoring"],"practices":[
{"code":"DSS05.01","name":"Protect against malicious software.","title":"Protection against malicious software","expectation":"Centrally managed malware protection (antivirus or EDR) is installed on all endpoints and servers; signature and engine updates are distributed automatically and users cannot disable the protection. Filtering is applied at email and web gateways, and detections are monitored in a central console and routed to the incident process; user awareness supports these technical measures.","design":"The auditor evaluates whether the protection policy covers all platforms, whether tamper protection is enabled and whether a central alerting workflow has been designed.","operating":"The asset inventory is compared with the agent list in the protection console to determine coverage and currency rates; for samples selected from detections during the period, the auditor verifies that they were handled through the incident process.","evidence":["Agent status and version report from the protection console","Coverage comparison against the asset inventory","Protection policy settings and tamper protection configuration","Detection alerts and related incident records"],"findings":["On some servers, the protection agent was not installed or had not reported to the central console for an extended period.","Users with local administrator rights were able to disable the protection."],"iso":["8.7","6.3"],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"DSS05.02","name":"Manage network and connectivity security.","title":"Network and connectivity security","expectation":"The network is segmented according to asset criticality, and inter-zone traffic is restricted by firewall rules based on the principle of least privilege. Rule changes go through the approved change process and the rule set is reviewed at intervals defined by the organization; remote access is protected by multi-factor authentication and sensitive data transfers are encrypted.","design":"The auditor evaluates whether the segmentation design isolates critical systems such as production databases and whether rule change and rule review processes are defined.","operating":"A firewall configuration extract is obtained in read-only mode and overly permissive rules are analyzed; samples selected from rule changes during the period are matched to change approvals, and evidence of the most recent rule review is inspected.","evidence":["Network topology and segmentation diagram","Firewall rule set extract and most recent review record","Rule change request and approval records","Remote access configuration and multi-factor authentication settings"],"findings":["The firewall contained overly permissive rules with no documented justification.","Multi-factor authentication was not enforced on some supplier remote access connections."],"iso":["8.20","8.21","8.22","8.24"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"access"},
{"code":"DSS05.03","name":"Manage endpoint security.","title":"Endpoint security and secure configuration","expectation":"Laptops, desktops, mobile devices and servers are built according to approved secure configuration standards; disk encryption, screen lock, restriction of local administrator rights and removable media controls are applied. Devices are enrolled in a central management tool, patches are deployed on a risk basis within timeframes defined by the organization, and deviations from the standard are monitored.","design":"The auditor evaluates whether configuration standards are approved and whether compliance is technically enforced and can be monitored through tools such as mobile device management or group policy.","operating":"A device compliance report is obtained from the central management tool and compared with the inventory; for a risk-based selection of devices, encryption, local administrator membership and patch status are inspected in read-only mode.","evidence":["Approved secure configuration standards","Device management tool compliance report","Disk encryption status report","Local administrators group membership list"],"findings":["Disk encryption was not enabled on some laptops.","A large number of users had local administrator rights without justification."],"iso":["8.1","8.9","7.9"],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"DSS05.04","name":"Manage user identity and logical access.","title":"User identity and logical access management","expectation":"Each user is issued an individual identity with a designated owner; access is defined based on job role and the principle of least privilege, with the approval of the data or system owner. For leavers and movers, access is removed or updated within a timeframe defined by the organization through an HR-driven trigger; privileged accounts are kept separate, protected by multi-factor authentication and their activity is monitored, and access is reviewed by system owners at intervals defined by the organization.","design":"The auditor evaluates whether the processes for granting, modifying, removing and reviewing access are designed to be linked to the HR source, approved and consistent with segregation of duties, and how shared, service and emergency accounts are managed.","operating":"The leavers list obtained from HR is compared with system user lists whose completeness has been validated, in order to identify active accounts belonging to leavers; for a risk-based selection of new access grants the approval trail is verified, for privileged accounts the justification is verified, and for the periodic review, reperformance is used to verify that access flagged for removal was actually removed.","evidence":["HR joiner, leaver and mover lists","User and entitlement lists for critical applications, databases and operating systems, with evidence that they were extracted from the system","Access request and approval records and evidence of periodic user access reviews","Privileged account inventory, privileged access management records and password and multi-factor authentication settings"],"findings":["Accounts of some leavers in a critical application remained active after their termination date, and some of these accounts had been used to log in.","The periodic user access review was performed on an incomplete user list, and access flagged for removal had not been changed in the system."],"iso":["5.15","5.16","5.18","8.2"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"access"},
{"code":"DSS05.05","name":"Manage physical access to I&T assets.","title":"Managing physical access to IT assets","expectation":"Physical access to data centers, server rooms and network cabinets is granted based on business need with the approval of the responsible manager, and is restricted through mechanisms such as badge access or biometric controls. Visitors are registered and escorted; access rights are removed upon departure and reviewed at intervals defined by the organization, and access logs and CCTV recordings are retained.","design":"The auditor evaluates whether the process for granting and removing physical access is linked to an HR trigger and whether sensitive areas are defined as separate access zones.","operating":"The list of individuals authorized to access the data center is obtained from the access control system and compared with the HR leavers list and business need; for samples selected from visitor logs, approval and escort are verified, and an on-site observation is performed.","evidence":["Data center authorized access list from the access control system","Physical access request and approval records","Visitor logs","Evidence of physical access review"],"findings":["Individuals with data center access included persons who no longer required it or who had left the organization.","Visit records for supplier technicians did not include escort details."],"iso":["7.1","7.2","7.3","7.4"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"access"},
{"code":"DSS05.06","name":"Manage sensitive documents and output devices.","title":"Protection of sensitive documents, output devices and media","expectation":"Rules for storage, inventory and handover consistent with classification exist for sensitive printed documents, valuable documents (such as checks, cards and certificates) and output devices. Secure printing with authentication is used on printers, media and documents are destroyed in a recorded and approved manner, and clean desk and clear screen rules are applied.","design":"The auditor evaluates whether rules for the storage, transport and destruction of sensitive documents and media, linked to the classification policy, have been defined.","operating":"The valuable documents inventory is reconciled to a physical count; samples selected from destruction records are inspected, and clean desk practice, printer trays and locked cabinets are checked through on-site observation.","evidence":["Valuable documents and sensitive media inventory with count records","Secure destruction records and certificates","Printer secure printing configuration","Clean desk and clear screen observation checklist"],"findings":["Unattended printouts containing personal data were observed in printer trays.","There was no evidence that decommissioned disks had been destroyed."],"iso":["5.13","7.7","7.10","7.14"],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"DSS05.07","name":"Manage vulnerabilities and monitor the infrastructure for security-related events.","title":"Vulnerability management and security event monitoring","expectation":"Infrastructure and applications are subject to authorized vulnerability scanning at a frequency defined by the organization; findings are prioritized by criticality and exposure and are either remediated within defined timeframes or covered by a risk acceptance. Security logs from critical systems are collected in a central system (such as a SIEM), protected against alteration, retained for a period defined by the organization and monitored using correlation rules, with suspicious events routed to the response process.","design":"The auditor evaluates whether scan scope, remediation timeframes and log sources cover critical assets, and whether monitoring rules include scenarios such as privileged account usage and failed logins.","operating":"The asset inventory is compared with the scan scope and SIEM log sources; for samples selected from critical vulnerabilities, remediation time and risk acceptances are verified, and for samples selected from alerts, the investigation and closure trail is verified. The auditor does not perform scans; existing results are inspected in read-only mode.","evidence":["Vulnerability scan reports and remediation tracking list","Vulnerability records covered by risk acceptance","SIEM log source list and retention settings","SIEM alerts and related investigation and incident records"],"findings":["Some critical vulnerabilities exceeded the remediation timeframe set by the organization and had no risk acceptance.","Logs from some critical database servers were not forwarded to the central monitoring system."],"iso":["8.8","8.15","8.16","5.25"],"clauses":["9.1"],"itgc":"operations"}
]},
{"objective":"DSS06","purpose":"Automated and manual controls embedded in business processes ensure that transactions are processed completely, accurately, with proper authorization and on time; financial reporting and operational decisions depend on the reliability of this data. Where controls are not designed in line with business objectives and risks, erroneous payments, duplicate entries or unauthorized transactions may accumulate undetected.","auditFocus":"The auditor examines end to end the input, processing and output controls (validation rules, matching, reconciliations, approval limits) and segregation of duties within business processes. Particular attention is given to the timely resolution of errors and exceptions, the traceability of transactions and the protection of automated control settings against unauthorized change.","keyRisks":["Incomplete or inaccurate data entry not being caught by validation controls and flowing through to the financial statements","A single individual being able to both initiate and approve a transaction due to segregation of duties conflicts","Exceptions and reconciliation differences accumulating without ownership"],"practices":[
{"code":"DSS06.01","name":"Align control activities embedded in business processes with enterprise objectives.","title":"Aligning business process controls with objectives and risks","expectation":"For critical business processes, a risk and control matrix exists showing process owners, key risks and the automated and manual controls addressing those risks. The control owner, frequency and evidence to be produced are defined; the matrix is updated upon process or system changes and control effectiveness is monitored by process owners.","design":"The auditor evaluates whether the risk and control matrix addresses critical risks and whether the balance between preventive and detective controls and the assignment of control ownership are appropriate.","operating":"End-to-end walkthroughs are performed for selected processes to verify that the controls in the matrix actually operate in practice; the auditor checks that the matrix was updated following process changes during the period.","evidence":["Process risk and control matrices","Process flowcharts and walkthrough notes","Control ownership and control self-assessment results","Matrix update records"],"findings":["Some controls in the matrix were no longer operating following a system change.","No control had been defined to address a critical process risk."],"iso":[],"clauses":["6.1","8.1"],"itgc":""},
{"code":"DSS06.02","name":"Control the processing of information.","title":"Information processing controls over input, processing and output","expectation":"Applications have defined input validation rules such as mandatory fields, format, value ranges and duplicate checks, record count and control total comparisons for interfaces and batch transfers, and defined critical calculations. Automated control settings are subject to change management; reconciliations between subledgers and the general ledger are performed at a frequency defined by the organization and reviewed by an independent person.","design":"The auditor evaluates whether completeness and accuracy controls have been designed for critical data flows and whether automated control configurations are protected against unauthorized change.","operating":"Automated controls are verified through configuration inspection and observation or reperformance in a test environment; where change controls are effective, reliance is placed on the configuration remaining unchanged throughout the period. For manual reconciliations, a risk-based sample is inspected to confirm that differences were investigated and approved.","evidence":["Application validation rules and configuration screenshots","Interface control total reports and rejected record lists","Subledger to general ledger reconciliations and approvals","Automated control configuration change records"],"findings":["Record count comparisons were not performed for interface transfers, and rejected records were not tracked.","Reconciliation differences were carried forward to the next period without explanation or approval."],"iso":[],"clauses":["8.1"],"itgc":""},
{"code":"DSS06.03","name":"Manage roles, responsibilities, access privileges and levels of authority.","title":"Business roles, approval authorities and segregation of duties","expectation":"An authority matrix showing business roles and approval limits has been approved by management, and application entitlements are mapped to these roles. Rules are defined for conflicting duties such as creating supplier records and approving payments; conflicts are prevented, compensating controls are applied to unavoidable conflicts, and access is reviewed by business owners at intervals defined by the organization.","design":"The auditor evaluates whether the conflict rule set covers critical transaction combinations and whether approval limits are enforced in the system.","operating":"Application entitlement data is obtained and the entire user population is analyzed against the conflict rule set using data analytics; for identified conflicts, the auditor inspects whether transactions were actually performed and whether compensating controls operated, and approval limits are reperformed on selected transactions.","evidence":["Approved authority matrix and approval limits","Application role-entitlement extract and user-role assignments","Segregation of duties conflict analysis results","Evidence of compensating controls and access review records"],"findings":["Some users had authority both to modify supplier master data and to approve payments.","Approval limits configured in the system did not agree with the approved authority matrix."],"iso":["5.3","5.15","5.18","8.2"],"clauses":["5.3"],"itgc":"access"},
{"code":"DSS06.04","name":"Manage errors and exceptions.","title":"Managing transaction errors and exceptions","expectation":"Transaction errors, rejected records and control exceptions such as limit breaches or unmatched invoices are logged, assigned to owners and corrected within timeframes defined by the organization. Corrections are approved and made in a manner that does not bypass the primary controls, and exception trends are analyzed for root causes.","design":"The auditor evaluates whether exception reports are generated, whether their owners are designated and whether corrections require approval.","operating":"Exception and error reports for the period are obtained and an aging analysis is performed; for selected exceptions, the appropriateness of the resolution, the approval and the correction method is inspected.","evidence":["Exception and error reports with aging information","Error correction records and approvals","Suspense account analyses","Exception trend analyses"],"findings":["Records rejected by the interface were held in suspense for extended periods without being corrected.","Error corrections were made directly in the database without approval and without leaving an audit trail."],"iso":[],"clauses":["10.2"],"itgc":""},
{"code":"DSS06.05","name":"Ensure traceability and accountability for information events.","title":"Transaction traceability and accountability","expectation":"For critical transactions, application audit trails record who did what and when, and who approved it; changes to master data and sensitive fields are monitored separately. Audit trails are protected against alteration and deletion, retained for a period defined by the organization, and transactions can be linked to their source documents.","design":"The auditor evaluates whether audit trail scope has been defined for critical tables and transactions and whether the trails are protected against unauthorized change.","operating":"Selected transactions are traced from source document to system record and from system record back to source document; audit trail settings are inspected in read-only mode, and approval is verified for samples selected from the master data change report.","evidence":["Application audit trail configuration","Master data change logs and approvals","Samples matching source documents to transactions","Log retention and protection settings"],"findings":["Change logging was disabled on critical master data tables.","Application administrators were able to delete audit trail records."],"iso":["8.15","5.33"],"clauses":["7.5"],"itgc":""},
{"code":"DSS06.06","name":"Secure information assets.","title":"Security of information assets within business processes","expectation":"Electronic and printed information used, transferred and stored in business processes is protected in accordance with its classification; measures such as masking, encryption and data loss prevention are applied to personal and confidential data. The process owner also keeps unstructured data sets, such as end-user spreadsheets and system exports, within the scope of control.","design":"The auditor evaluates whether information classification is reflected in process-level protection rules and whether critical end-user computing tools have been identified.","operating":"In selected processes, export, sharing and storage points are inspected; access and version controls over critical end-user computing tools and evidence of encryption for sensitive data transfers are verified, and masked samples are used for personal data during the review.","evidence":["Information classification policy and process-level implementation rules","Inventory of critical end-user computing tools and related controls","Data loss prevention policies and incident reports","Sensitive data transfer methods and encryption settings"],"findings":["Spreadsheets used in financial reporting were held in a shared folder without access restrictions or version control.","Exports containing personal data were sent by email without encryption."],"iso":["5.12","5.14","8.11","8.12"],"clauses":[],"itgc":""}
]}
]
);
